Third-party material
====================

This repository does not redistribute any skill it did not author.

What is here
------------

corpus/            Twelve skills written for this project as a controlled test corpus, with
                   redundancy planted by construction. Authored by the repository owner and
                   covered by the MIT License, like the rest of the repository. This is also
                   the corpus the demo index is built from.

build/             A demo index built from corpus/ only.

results/           Machine-readable results: scores, token counts, statistics. Numbers and
                   condition labels, no skill text.

paper/             The article. CC BY 4.0; see LICENSE-PAPER.

What is deliberately absent
---------------------------

The production study in the paper used a 45-skill library that ships with Anthropic's Claude
products. Seventeen of those skills carry their own license header; six are marked
"Proprietary. LICENSE.txt has complete terms". Those documents belong to their authors and are
not ours to publish, and neither are works derived closely from them: the atoms produced by the
refactoring step reproduce their lessons verbatim by design, and the experiment prompts embed
whole skill documents inline.

So the following are not in this repository:

  - the text of any skill from that library
  - the atoms refactored from those skills
  - the agent prompts and generated deliverables from the production study, which embed them

What is here instead is everything needed to rebuild them locally from your own installation:
docs/SKILLS-MANIFEST.json lists every skill used, with the SHA-256 of the SKILL.md the study
read, so you can confirm you are looking at the same documents. `skillmerge discover` and
`skillmerge jobs` then regenerate the refactoring prompts, and `skillmerge build` produces the
index. See docs/reproducing.md.

If you maintain a skill library of your own, point the pipeline at it. Nothing in the method
depends on the particular corpus.
